# The first lesson: a take-off in a light aeroplane, from lining up to five
# hundred feet.
#
#   name TEXT...                   what the lesson is called
#   teaches CLASS                  which class of aircraft it is for
#   stage TEXT...                  a stage, in the order flown
#   do TEXT...                     what to do at this stage
#   until PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE     what ends the stage
#   hold PROPERTY LOW HIGH TEXT... a band that must hold for the whole stage
#   need PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE TEXT...  what must be true by the stage's end
#
# with `#` beginning a comment; src/sim/lesson.hpp owns the format.
#
# **The figures are the class's, not one aeroplane's, and they are therefore
# loose.** A lesson teaches a class (REQUIREMENTS.md 4.3), and the four light
# aircraft here rotate between about 34 knots in the Cub and about 55 in the
# 182, so a band that suits all four cannot be tight around any of them. The
# rotation figure below is the lowest of them less a margin: it catches an
# aeroplane hauled off the ground well before its speed, which is the fault
# this lesson is for, and it will not catch a Cessna rotating five knots
# early. A per-aeroplane lesson would be tighter, and the format already
# allows one - `teaches` would become an aircraft - but the item asks for a
# lesson a class, so this is a lesson a class.
#
# **The throttle is a `need` and not a `hold`**, because "open the throttle
# smoothly to full" means it is not full at the first instant and should not
# be. A band judged from the first tick of the roll would fault every
# by-the-book take-off there is. It is judged where the teaching judges it: by
# the time she leaves the ground.
#
# **The attitude band is measured, not guessed.** Flown by the book - by the
# take-off autopilot, on the figures each aeroplane publishes - the four light
# aircraft hold between -4.5 and 19.4 degrees once off the ground: the Cub
# -2.5 to 18.3, the PA-28 -3.0 to 18.7, the 172 -3.9 to 19.1 and the 182 -4.5
# to 19.4. The nose-down figures are the moment after lift-off, as she is let
# settle into the climb. The band below is that range with about four degrees
# of margin at each end, which passes every correctly flown take-off in the
# class and still catches an aeroplane hauled into a steep climb or pushed
# over into a dive.
#
# **What this lesson cannot catch, said where it matters.** "Rotating early"
# is the fault a take-off lesson most wants to name, and the figure below
# catches it only for the slowest aeroplane in the class. A 172 hauled off at
# 47 knots, against the 55 or so it should use, is a long way early and sails
# past a threshold set for a Cub. Catching that needs the lesson to be able to
# say "her own rotation speed" rather than a number - the figure is already in
# every aeroplane's `assets/figures/<id>.xml`, and `sim::departure_speeds`
# already reads it - and the format has no way to say it. Until it has, what
# catches a badly flown rotation here is the attitude band, not the speed.

name Take-off in a light aeroplane
teaches light-aircraft

stage The roll
do Line up on the centreline, then open the throttle smoothly to full
do Keep straight with the rudder: she will swing left as the power comes in
do Let the speed build. Do not hurry her off the ground
need fcs/throttle-cmd-norm >= 0.90 Open the throttle fully for the take-off
need velocities/vc-kts >= 32 Let her reach the rotation speed before easing the nose up
until position/h-agl-ft >= 15

stage The initial climb
do Hold the climbing attitude and let the speed settle
do Keep the wings level and the ball centred
hold attitude/theta-deg -8.0 24.0 Hold the climbing attitude: the nose wandered
until position/h-agl-ft >= 200

stage Climbing away
do Climb straight ahead to five hundred feet before turning
hold velocities/vc-kts 45.0 120.0 Hold the climbing speed
until position/h-agl-ft >= 500
